.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="After Maiman&#x27;s laser it became clear that real laser beams are neither idealized geometric rays nor infinite plane waves, but Gaussian beams. Kogelnik and Li&#x27;s 1966 paper showed that a Gaussian beam is completely characterized by one complex beam parameter q(z), and, crucially, that q transforms through any sequence of lenses, mirrors, or free-space sections by the same ABCD ray-transfer matrix already used for geometric rays -- unifying ray optics and Gaussian-beam wave optics under one formalism. propagate_q implements q&#x27; = (Aq+B)/(Cq+D), and q_to_beam_params recovers the physical beam radius w(z) at any plane.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/optics/gaussian_beams/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_gaussian_beam_through_lens_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/optics/gaussian_beams/plot_gaussian_beam_through_lens`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Kogelnik and Li: focusing a Gaussian beam through a thin lens</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/optics/gaussian_beams/plot_gaussian_beam_through_lens`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Carl Friedrich Gauss showed that any centered optical system, however many lenses and refracting surfaces it contains, can be fully characterized in the paraxial approximation by linear algebra rather than surface-by-surface trigonometry: every elementary optical element is a linear map on the ray state (y, \theta) -- height above the axis and angle to it --">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/optics/ray_optics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_abcd_system_matrix_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/optics/ray_optics/plot_abcd_system_matrix`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Gauss's ABCD formalism: a composite system as a matrix product</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/optics/ray_optics/plot_abcd_system_matrix`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Charles Kao and George Hockham&#x27;s insight that glass fiber could in principle be purified to carry light over kilometers launched the fiber-optic communications industry. The graded-index (GRIN) fiber profile that followed guides light by continuously bending rays back toward the core axis, rather than by a single sharp core-cladding interface: for the standard quadratic radial index profile n(r) = n_0(1 - n_2 r^2/2), paraxial rays oscillate sinusoidally about the axis instead of walking off it. grin_medium builds the ABCD matrix of exactly this profile; chaining it through OpticalSystem and calling trace_ray reproduces the self-confining ray trajectory below, contrasted against the same ray walking away from the axis in a homogeneous medium.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/optics/ray_optics/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_grin_fiber_guiding_thumb.png
